 "action_summary": "A report merged onto main earlier on 2026-11-12 stated that 10 of 605 declared predicates carry an expectation, 1.7 percent of the corpus. That is wrong by exactly a factor of two. The binary-identity corpus is read by two code paths inside one function: an explicit branch, which is the only place an entry's name survives, and the generic JSON walk, because predicate_of also accepts the arg key. Each of its five predicates was therefore yielded twice. The corrected measurement is 5 of 600, 0.83 percent, and declaring_none is unchanged at 595 because every duplicated entry was one of the checked ones, so the double count inflated the numerator of the only flattering fraction in the document. All five checked predicates are tar, git, python3, node and jq --version: identity checks on installed tools, so not one of the 600 declared verifications in this repository asserts anything about the work the model did. This round supersedes that number rather than editing it, and adds the gate whose absence let it ship: a static declaration floor measured in 1.9 seconds that executes nothing and writes nothing, a committed baseline, a ratchet with three verdicts, and a pull_request workflow. The obvious gate was rejected on measurement: --audit takes about 13 minutes, writes into the tree it measures, and 8 of its 58 failures name another machine's filesystem while 19 predicates land in the 30 second timeout budget, so it is machine dependent and would be flaky in CI, and a flaky gate gets muted, which is worse than no gate because it manufactures the appearance of a constraint.",
